 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection in MATLAB, you can use the `imgaussfilt` function to smooth the image and then apply the Sobel operator. Here's how you can modify your code:
```matlab
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter to reduce noise
gaussian_filtered_image = imgaussfilt(double(gray_image), 3); % Standard deviation of 3 pixels
% Define Sobel operator in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x and y directions
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display original image, gaussian filtered image, and edge image
subplot(1,3,1), imshow(input_image), title('Original Image');
subplot(1,3,2), imshow(gaussian_filtered_image), title('Gaussian Filtered Image');
subplot(1,3,3), imshow(edge_image), title('Edge Image');
```
In this modified code, we first apply a Gaussian filter using the `imgaussfilt` function with a standard deviation of 3 pixels. The resulting smoothed image is then used as the input for the Sobel edge detection algorithm. The rest of the code remains the same as your original implementation.

