2. Install bind9 DNS server: Bind9 is a widely used open-source DNS server software that allows you to manage your own DNS records. Installing it on your Linux machine will enable you to map your domain name to your local IP address.

   For Ubuntu, run the following command in a terminal:
   ```
   sudo apt update
   sudo apt install bind9 bind9utils bind9-doc
   ```

3. Configure bind9: Open the primary named.conf file located at /etc/bind/named.conf in a text editor as root:
   ```
   sudo nano /etc/bind/named.conf
   ```

   Add the following lines inside the "options" statement:
   ```
   directory "/var/cache/bind";
   allow-transfer { none; };
   dnssec-validation auto;

   zone "." IN {
       type hint; file "/etc/bind/db.root";
   };

   // Add this at the end of the file
   zone "<yourdomain.com>" {
       type master;
       file "/etc/bind/db.<yourdomain.com>";
   };
   ```

   Replace `<yourdomain.com>` with your actual domain name. Save and exit the text editor.

4. Create a new zone file for your domain: Create the file /etc/bind/db.<yourdomain.com> with the following content:
