 It seems you're encountering an issue with making an API request while including an Authorization header, and you suspect the issue might be related to the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header being set to "*" or a wildcard in the server's response. Let's clarify this common misconception.

The 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header is part of the Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) mechanism, which controls which websites can make requests to your server. Setting it to "*" or a wildcard allows any origin to access your resources. This doesn't directly affect the inclusion of the Authorization header in your request.

However, when making cross-origin requests, browsers enforce certain security rules known as the Same Origin Policy. One of these rules is that the request must not include credentials like cookies or the Authorization header unless the server explicitly permits it by setting 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials' to true in its response. So even if you have 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' set to "*", you still need to ensure the server allows the inclusion of credentials by setting 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials' to true.

If you're working with JavaScript in a browser environment, you can check for both headers using the XMLHttpRequest object or Fetch API. For example:

```javascript
