 To prevent your planets from being generated too close to each other, you can modify the `generateRandomPlanets` function by keeping track of the minimum distance between the newly generated planet and the existing ones. Here's an updated version of the function:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;
  let minDistances = []; // Keep track of the minimum distances for each dimension

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    let tooClose = false;

    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const existingPlanet = planets[i];
      const distanceSquared = planet.position.map((_, dim) => {
        const coord = existingPlanet.position[dim];
        const dist = Math.pow(planet.position[dim] - coord, 2);
        minDistances[dim] = Math.min(dist, minDistances[dim] || dist);
        return dist;
      });

      const distance = Math.sqrt(distanceSquared[0] + distanceSquared[1] + distanceSquared[2]);

      if (distance < minDistance) {
        tooClose = true;
        break;
      }
    }

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
}
```

This updated version uses an array called `minDistances` to keep track of the minimum distances for each dimension. It updates these distances as it checks for collisions and uses them to calculate the overall distance between the newly generated planet and

 To create a React utility for removing widows in text, you can utilize the `react-text` library that provides fine-grained control over text rendering and allows customizing the behavior of line breaking. Here's an example of how to implement a custom component for removing widows using this library:

1. First, install the `react-text` library by running the following command in your terminal:

```bash
npm install react-text
```

2. Create a new file named `WidowRemover.js` and add the following code:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import Text from 'react-text';

const WidowRemover = ({ children }) => {
  const lines = React.useMemo(
    () => (
      children.toString().split(/[\r\n]+/)
    ),
    [children]
  );

  return (
    <Text>
      {lines.map((line, index) => {
        if (index !== lines.length - 1 && /[^\s]+$/.test(line)) {
          // Add a non-breaking space at the end of the line to prevent widows
          return `${line} ${'\u00A0'} `;
        }
        return line;
      })}
    </Text>
  );
};

export default WidowRemover;
```

This component splits the given text into lines, checks for the last line, and adds a non-breaking space (U+00A0) if it doesn't end with a whitespace character to prevent widows.

